+## Bound the holder, not only the waiter: flock's payload inherits the lock fd (agents-etvw)
+
+`flock` gives the lock to the WHOLE process tree, not to the command that asked for it: the payload INHERITS the
+lock fd, so a `git send-pack` grandchild stuck on the network still holds the VM-wide lock after the tool that took
+it has gone. Observed 2026-10-14: a `--force-with-lease` stuck in send-pack, every other lane's `bd` call queued
+behind `flock -w 300`, and a bounded command dying at its ceiling with one rc 124 after 240s.
+**Driven both ways**: WITHOUT `-o` the lock is STILL HELD after the payload exits; WITH `-o` it is FREE. So the rule
+is BOUND THE HOLDER, NOT ONLY THE WAITER - do not rely on the waiter's timeout to release a lock a grandchild holds.
+In practice: network subcommands run under `timeout -k`; BOTH paths pass `-o`; and on timeout the tool says LOUDLY
+that THE WRITE DID NOT LAND, that the lock IS released, and that the rule is BACK OFF AND RETRY, KILL NOTHING. `124`
+stays the fleet's "unknown, not a pass" - a killed or timed-out command never reads as success.
